WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.294
Express and other Node.

00:00:02.294 --> 00:00:10.428
js frameworks make it easy to receive request data, but they do not automatically know the application's rules.

00:00:10.428 --> 00:00:17.311
Request bodies, path parameters, query parameters, headers, cookies, and file metadata all need explicit expectations.

00:00:17.311 --> 00:00:25.445
Middleware can help centralize validation, but each route should still have a clear contract for what it accepts.

00:00:25.445 --> 00:00:29.408
Frontend form validation should align with server validation.

00:00:29.408 --> 00:00:37.959
A form might enforce required fields, length, and format for usability, while the API enforces the same rules authoritatively.

00:00:37.959 --> 00:00:47.762
When frontend and backend rules diverge, users get confusing errors and attackers get opportunities to submit data the server did not expect.

00:00:47.762 --> 00:00:51.725
Shared schema patterns can help reduce that drift.

00:00:51.725 --> 00:00:54.853
URL and route parameters need context.

00:00:54.853 --> 00:01:02.987
A value that looks like an identifier may still need to match the authenticated user's tenant or permissions.

00:01:02.987 --> 00:01:09.453
Headers and cookies may require format checks, signature verification, expiration checks, and safe parsing.

00:01:09.453 --> 00:01:17.170
Uploaded files need validation of size, name, declared type, detected content, storage location, and later access behavior.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:22.384
GraphQL and WebSocket inputs deserve the same care as REST APIs.

00:01:22.384 --> 00:01:28.015
GraphQL schemas describe operations, but authorization and business rules still need enforcement.

00:01:28.015 --> 00:01:35.732
WebSocket messages are long-lived external input and should be parsed, typed, size-limited, and validated by message type.

00:01:35.732 --> 00:01:39.278
Every protocol boundary is a validation boundary.
